^^How to say 'no'^^

Now we will learn the imperative, i.e. how to say no. Negation is easy because it uses the same verb as the imperative from the last skill.

The Finnish word for no, *ei*, is actually a verb that needs to be conjugated like other verbs. Here's how you conjugate it in different persons.
	
Minä en ole	I am not
Sinä et ole	You are not
Hän ei ole	He/she is not
Me emme ole	We are not
Te ette ole	You (pl.) are not
He eivät ole	They are not

The main verb is conjugated as in first person present tense, minus the *n*, so the same as the singular imperative form!	

Minä en juokse.	
Te ette halua.	

When you want to answer a question with 'no', there are multiple ways of doing so:

Sataako ulkona? (Is it raining outside?)
Ei. (No.)
Ei sada. (It doesn't rain.)

To recap the questions:

Ei*kö* ulkona sada? (Isn't it raining outside?)
Kyllä. (Yes.)
Ei. (No.)
Sataa. (Rains.)
Ei sada. (Doesn't rain.)

Note: When asking 'doesn't it...' the word 'ei' is the one that gets the question suffix *-ko/-kö*, not the other verb.

Use the nominative + the *-ko/-kö* -ending (sataa*ko*, tulet*ko*, menet*kö*) in *positive* sentences, but use the imperative (sada, tule, mene) in *negative* sentences and add the *-ko/-kö* -ending to 'ei' instead.